UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 17, Lesson 1: Electric circuits', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy does a lamp stay off if one wire is disconnected from the battery?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDraw and label three circuits: working closed; open switch; missing wire. Predict lamp on/off for each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Draw and label three circuits: working closed; open switch; missing wire. Predict lamp on/off for each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. Reasoning: I used circuit by attending to path rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Correction: The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. Why: the correction restores accurate use of circuit.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses circuit. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about circuit, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link. / Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. This matches the lesson skill for circuit.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.","Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Why does a lamp stay off if one wire is disconnected from the battery?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path.","An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop.","The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.","Careful use of path with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named path, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the path used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising circuit. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation.","Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone.","Decide from which object looks most exciting."],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, glass marble, fallen dry leaf. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path.","Named observation plus how it supports the claim.","A longer story with no observation.","A label copied from the title with no evidence."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.","Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path."],"question":"Using this idea — An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a… — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster.","Stop early when you like the first result.","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer.","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence.","Repeat the worked example objects unchanged.","Invent a dramatic result with no observation.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses circuit. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. Reasoning: I used circuit by attending to path rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about circuit, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Correction: The path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop. Why: the correction restores accurate use of circuit.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w17-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise circuit.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Physical World","success_criteria":["I can use circuit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify path and explain why it matters.","I can practise component independently on a new prompt.","I can notice closed needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBattery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy does a lamp stay off if one wire is disconnected from the battery?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.","explain":"An electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Electric circuits.","caption":"Need a complete loop.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Closed path</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">loop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Open gap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lamp off</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Battery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path. Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Battery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path. Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBattery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Closed path model.","caption":"Three circuit drawings.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Battery wire lamp</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">labels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">electricity sprays out</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Here is a more productive model: Battery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe path is open/incomplete so current cannot flow around the loop.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking electricity “sprays out” of one wire into the air misrepresents the need for a closed path. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about circuit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn electric circuit needs a closed path for current: typically a power source, conductors and a component (lamp/buzzer) in a complete loop. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 17, Lesson 1: Electric circuits","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Electricity and energy","week":17,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nBattery → wire → lamp → wire → battery. A gap (open switch) stops the lamp. A closed switch completes the path.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Electric circuits.","caption":"Need a complete loop.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Closed path</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">loop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Open gap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lamp off</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Closed path model.","caption":"Three circuit drawings.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Battery wire lamp</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">labels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">electricity sprays out</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-sci-y4-w17-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
